  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Unseasonably mild through Friday with 2.5 weeks-\nlong dry spell coming to an end late tonight into Thursday \n\nAnomalous upper level ridging and south-southwest flow aloft\nwill maintain unseasonably warm temperatures through late week.\nMax/Min temps will be 10 to 20 degrees above the historical \naverage for the first week of November, but should fall short of\ndaily record highs (see climate section). \n\nA leading frontal band of light rain should reach the western \nAlleghenies around or shortly after midnight and continue \nspreading eastward across central PA through Thursday morning. \nFcst rainfall amounts are 0.25\" inch or less through 00Z Fri in\nmost areas and will end the 2.5 weeks-long dry spell. \n\nThe front slows/stalls near the I95 corridor Thursday night. A \nshortwave trough/closed H5 low dropping southeast from the Great\nLakes is expected to reinvigorate precipitation along the \nnearly stationary frontal zone on Friday which could clip the \nfar southeast zones/Lower Susquehanna Valley. Model trends have \nbeen shifting this rain farther to the east/closer to the coast \nover the past few cycles. A dry slot may keep most locations dry\nThursday afternoon into Friday following the initial period of \nrain late tonight through Thursday morning. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Trending cooler this weekend with light rain \ndownwind of Lake Erie  \n\nSeasonably cooler/breezy pattern to start the first full \nweekend of November. The passage of closed H5 low followed by \nmean cyclonic flow favors persistent clouds and periods of lake\nenhanced rain over the western and central Alleghenies on \nSaturday. We were keen to manually adjust max temps a few\ndegrees lower on 11/7 to account for the cooler pattern shift\nwhich looks to continue into early next week.",
